{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547645-demon-the-fallen-and-devil-s-due-compatibility","id":1547645,"title":"Demon The Fallen and Devil's Due compatibility","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-01-09T06:12:09-08:00","id":1547645,"title":"Demon The Fallen and Devil's Due compatibility","content":"I recently learned that Dark Ages Vampire book Devil's Due has also a Demon (and infernalist thrall) creation in it that is somewhat connected to Demon The Fallen. I also heard that with that creation one can make a playable Earthbound.<br /> <br /> I was wondering would it be mechanically compatible and balanced (well balanced in WoD standards) to create an &quot;Earthbound&quot; like character for the use in a Demon The Fallen game where others play default fallens? Or would it be mechanically worse, better or weird on soma way?<br /> <br /> I was checking Devil's Due because the actual Earthbound creation in the Earthbound book sounds crazy powerful even if they don't have bodies. I'm mostly looking for playing a &quot;playable&quot; Earthbound or a thrall of an Earthbound who will not be so outclassed by the other Fallen characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27895-majordeckard","name":"MajorDeckard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27895_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-09T11:56:23-08:00","id":1547648,"title":"","content":"A small nitpick: While most books for the Revised Dark Ages line are treated as Vampire supplements, this is really not the case like it was in the 2e Dark Ages books. Dark Ages Vampire, the book, was just the only fully published version with the core rules in it, where all the other books don't have the basics like dice rolling and general character creation and all of that (similar to how the first edition of the new World of Darkness, now Chronicles of Darkness, didn't repeat the core rules in every character book). White Wolf did eventually put out a free digital copy of those rules so you didn't need to buy Vampire to play DA Werewolf, Mage, Fae, Inquisitor, or smaller supplements like Devil's Due; all of which can work with a Vampire game, or be run as their own thing.<br /> <br /> Devil's Due is a lot more than somewhat connected to Demon the Fallen. The demons detailed in it are the same entities. But, different times means things worked different for them (Mage and Fae got similarly disparate rules from their modern day characters), so the rules are less compatible than some people might like.<br /> <br /> A big difference is that in the Dark Medieval (this is a phrase coined to make up for the fact that the DA books have always drastically stretched the common definition of the post-Roman dark age well into the early Medieval period) there is no crack in the Abyss that demons are leaking out of. All demons on Earth had to be summoned in some fashion. This means most are kept from being pulled right back into the Abyss by whatever binding rituals were used in their summoning. Unsurprisingly, they don't want to get sent back, so they start looking for ways to stick around if whatever magic used in their summoning ends and their tether to the world breaks. Becoming an Earthbound is one option for that, and one Devil's Due does let people start with (though it's not exactly great for group play). The big problem with playing an Earthbound is... you're stuck in an object and have to rely on other people to actually do much unless it's right in front of you. There's benefits too, enough that you don't get any modifications to what level you start as in Devil's Due if you pick to start Earthbound, so it's definitely &quot;weird&quot; more than worse or better.<br /> <br /> Without serious house-ruling, the Devil's Due rules aren't going to mesh easily with the Demon the Fallen rules. The broad strokes are there, but you're going to quickly run into a bunch of similar but also different rules. It's not impossible by any means, but I don't think it's less work than just using DtF: Earthbound and making playable earthbound by just giving them less toys to start with.<br /> <br /> While probably a bit more work, IMO the best thing to do if you want playable Earthbound in a mixed group, is for the Earthbound players to also have a primary Thrall that they play when the group is out doing stuff. There's a lot of the classic Shadowrun &quot;Rigger Problem&quot; when you do that sort of thing, but the pay off is something that can be functional in-play as the Thrall can actually walk around and go out and do stuff under their master's commands (with the perks of being a Thrall) and being weaker &quot;in the field&quot; than normal Fallen is counterbalanced by how potent you are if the team gets attacked on their home turf.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-09T16:27:28-08:00","id":1547653,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1547645-demon-the-fallen-and-devil-s-due-compatibility?p=1547648#post1547648\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A small nitpick: While most books for the Revised Dark Ages line are treated as Vampire supplements, this is really not the case like it was in the 2e Dark Ages books. Dark Ages Vampire, the book, was just the only fully published version with the core rules in it, where all the other books don't have the basics like dice rolling and general character creation and all of that (similar to how the first edition of the new World of Darkness, now Chronicles of Darkness, didn't repeat the core rules in every character book). White Wolf did eventually put out a free digital copy of those rules so you didn't need to buy Vampire to play DA Werewolf, Mage, Fae, Inquisitor, or smaller supplements like Devil's Due; all of which can work with a Vampire game, or be run as their own thing.<br /> <br /> Devil's Due is a lot more than somewhat connected to Demon the Fallen. The demons detailed in it are the same entities. But, different times means things worked different for them (Mage and Fae got similarly disparate rules from their modern day characters), so the rules are less compatible than some people might like.<br /> <br /> A big difference is that in the Dark Medieval (this is a phrase coined to make up for the fact that the DA books have always drastically stretched the common definition of the post-Roman dark age well into the early Medieval period) there is no crack in the Abyss that demons are leaking out of. All demons on Earth had to be summoned in some fashion. This means most are kept from being pulled right back into the Abyss by whatever binding rituals were used in their summoning. Unsurprisingly, they don't want to get sent back, so they start looking for ways to stick around if whatever magic used in their summoning ends and their tether to the world breaks. Becoming an Earthbound is one option for that, and one Devil's Due does let people start with (though it's not exactly great for group play). The big problem with playing an Earthbound is... you're stuck in an object and have to rely on other people to actually do much unless it's right in front of you. There's benefits too, enough that you don't get any modifications to what level you start as in Devil's Due if you pick to start Earthbound, so it's definitely &quot;weird&quot; more than worse or better.<br /> <br /> Without serious house-ruling, the Devil's Due rules aren't going to mesh easily with the Demon the Fallen rules. The broad strokes are there, but you're going to quickly run into a bunch of similar but also different rules. It's not impossible by any means, but I don't think it's less work than just using DtF: Earthbound and making playable earthbound by just giving them less toys to start with.<br /> <br /> While probably a bit more work, IMO the best thing to do if you want playable Earthbound in a mixed group, is for the Earthbound players to also have a primary Thrall that they play when the group is out doing stuff. There's a lot of the classic Shadowrun &quot;Rigger Problem&quot; when you do that sort of thing, but the pay off is something that can be functional in-play as the Thrall can actually walk around and go out and do stuff under their master's commands (with the perks of being a Thrall) and being weaker &quot;in the field&quot; than normal Fallen is counterbalanced by how potent you are if the team gets attacked on their home turf.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for the answer! Yeah the lore implication of Devil's Due Demons can be from Earthbound with their ritual of possession who was on Earth and bounded and not just beamed straight out of the Abyss in modern times is an interesting thing to play as a character. But apart from that, mechanical side sounds like it would be a mess. I heard the overall Demon rules in Devil's Due is better than the ones in DtF though and I'm curious as a person who loves DtF but don't have any experience with Devil's Due outside of recently checking the section of character creation.<br /> <br /> Playing a thrall of an Earthbound and rare cases playing the actual Earthbound seems to be the way to go. It balances in somewhat because most of the time you're weaker than the other players but easily replaceable and in some rare occasions you're so much more powerful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27895-majordeckard","name":"MajorDeckard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27895_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-09T19:23:53-08:00","id":1547658,"title":"","content":"Devil's Due rules (keeping in mind they have the benefit of hindsight) are generally better; though it mostly comes out in the way demonic powers are constructed. You're not going to get stuck with DtF's wildly inconsistent power level between each of the Lores. Though really beyond that the end result isn't that different. Though there's also less material to work with as a much smaller book with no supplements, so the powers (for Thralls and infernalists getting buffs too) are also fairly truncated from full DtF rules.<br /> <br /> If I was running a shorter game, I'd probably reach for Devil's Due (also DA Revised base rules are significantly better than the rest of the Revised ones IMO, so there's that going for it as well), but if I were planning on something where the players are likely to earn a significant chunk of XP over the course of play, I'd lean towards DtF for having more meat on the bones in that regard.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-01-10T04:01:11-08:00","id":1547662,"title":"","content":"Ok this is a really good advice. Thank you! I'll just take the lore bits from it like the Sundered ritual and whatnot and probably stick with the DtF. I'll have to read the Arcana powers thoroughly again though since if they have anything that sounds better or that DtF don't have, it can be implemented I'm guessing fairly easily.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27895-majordeckard","name":"MajorDeckard","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27895_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}